I say that {B} is also a cube.

Since {C} and {D} are cubes, {C} and {D} are similar solid numbers, therefore two mean proportional numbers fall between {C} and {D}. [!just VIII.19]

Since as many numbers fall in continued proportion between those which have the same ratio with {C} and {D} as fall between {C} and {D}, therefore two mean proportional numbers {E} and {F} fall between {A} and {B}. [!just VIII.18]

Since, then, the four numbers {A}, {E}, {F}, and {B} are in continued proportion, and {A} is a cube, therefore {B} is also a cube. [!just VIII.23]

Therefore, *if two numbers have to one another the ratio which a cubic number has to a cubic number, and the first is a cube, then the second is also a cube.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition is analogous to the previous one about squares. Its proof is straightforward.

This proposition is used in @IX.10.
